Networking Fundamentals and Basic Reconnaissance:

Networking concepts are important in cybersecurity because computers, servers, and other devices communicate through networks.
An IP address is a unique address used to identify a device and enable communication over a network. 
The Domain Name System (DNS) translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses so that users can access network services easily.

A port is a logical communication endpoint used to identify specific services or applications on a device.
Common examples include port 80 for HTTP, port 443 for HTTPS, port 22 for SSH, and port 53 for DNS.
Protocols are sets of rules that define how devices communicate. Common networking protocols include HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, UDP, and DNS.

Basic reconnaissance is the process of gathering publicly available information about a system or domain before authorized security testing.
Common tools and commands include ping for checking network connectivity, nslookup for obtaining DNS information,
tracert or traceroute for examining the network path to a destination, and whois for viewing available domain registration information.

Network discovery tools such as Nmap can also be used in an authorized environment to identify hosts and examine available network services.
These activities should only be performed on systems that are owned by the user or where explicit permission has been granted.

Conclusion:
Understanding IP addresses, DNS, ports, and protocols provides a strong foundation for cybersecurity. Basic reconnaissance helps
security professionals understand an authorized target environment and gather relevant information before performing security assessments.
